sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control--claude-opus-5-5--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Mesh statistics of radiolarian lattice shells against foam and two Voronoi nulls" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> ;
    dct:description "Reuse the cell-geometry pipeline (Cellpose segmentation, junction-angle and neighbour-count analysis, synthetic controls) on images of latticed radiolarian shells. Primary data must be micrographs (SEM or light), because Haeckel's plates are idealized drawings; use them only as a secondary, explicitly flagged set. Measure in the central region of each shell where foreshortening is below 10% (or correct projection on a fitted sphere): fraction of trivalent vertices, vertex-angle SD around 120 degrees, polygon-side distribution and its second moment mu2, Lewis's law slope (pore area versus sides) and Aboav-Weaire. Compare to three references fixed in advance: a relaxed foam (Plateau borders, simulated by vertex model at matched polydispersity), a Poisson-Voronoi null, and a hard-core (equal-sized random packing) Voronoi null. The hard-core null is essential: equal vesicle packing alone yields many hexagons without surface tension, so only vertex angles and mu2 at matched polydispersity can separate surface-tension moulding from packing. Euler's relation forces mean sides near 6 on any trivalent net, so mean side number is excluded as evidence (the lesson of the repo's ch07-10 result)." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "For at least 30 micrograph-imaged shells, vertex-angle SD and mu2 significantly closer to the matched foam reference than to both Poisson and hard-core Voronoi nulls (or not), with segmentation error bounded by synthetic-lattice calibration." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Segmentation of 3D lattice shells in 2D images too unreliable (calibration error larger than the foam-versus-hard-core gap), or fewer than 30 licensed micrographs." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Scout image sources with licences: mikrotax radiolaria pages, the Tetard et al. 2020 radiolarian image workflow data, Wikimedia Commons, and open-access SEM figures; record species, mineral and magnification. 2. Pre-register: at least 30 shells, the central-region rule, the metrics, the nulls, and the match criterion (vertex-angle SD and mu2 closer to the foam reference than to both Voronoi nulls, by bootstrap over shells). 3. Segment pores (Cellpose via Nebius, or classical watershed if Cellpose fails on lattice images; compare both). 4. Simulate the three references at each shell's measured pore-size polydispersity. 5. Report per shell and pooled; publish as a claim test on thompson1917-ch09-14." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: A licensed set of high-resolution micrographs of latticed shells; Cellpose was trained on cells, not mineral lattices, so segmentation may need a custom method." ;
    skos:note "Tool: cellpose" ;
    skos:note "Tool: nebius" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: cell-geometry-pipeline" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-14> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-15> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.mikrotax.org/radiolaria/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Kunstformen_der_Natur> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-16-2415-2020> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1080/00107518408210979> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How much of biomineral form is physical, and how much is cell-controlled?" .

  <https://www.mikrotax.org/radiolaria/> dct:title "mikrotax Radiolaria" ;
    dct:description "Taxonomic database with radiolarian images; image licences vary by source and must be checked per image." .
  <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Kunstformen_der_Natur> dct:title "Kunstformen der Natur (Haeckel), Wikimedia Commons" ;
    dct:description "Public-domain plates; secondary, flagged as idealized drawings." .
  <https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-16-2415-2020> dct:title "Technical note: A new automated radiolarian image acquisition, stacking, processing, segmentation and identification workflow" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Tetard et al. 2020, A new automated radiolarian image acquisition, stacking, processing, segmentation and identification workflow" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1080/00107518408210979> dct:title "Soap, cells and statistics—random patterns in two dimensions" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Weaire and Rivier 1984, Soap, cells and statistics: random patterns in two dimensions" .
}

